When you migrate an application, it's tempting to rebuild what's already there. The structure works, the users know it, and nobody wants to explain why things look different. But a migration is also the best chance you'll get to ask a question the original team may never have asked: do we still need this?

On one of my OneStream migrations, that question removed a yearly manual chore completely. Here's what we found and what we built instead.

## The legacy design

The existing application had a very rigid time hierarchy: Year, then Month, then Week, then Day. Every day was a member, every day rolled up to a week, and every week rolled up to a month.

The catch is that weeks and months don't line up. A week can straddle two months, and the calendar shifts a little every year. So at the end of every year, someone had to open the time hierarchy and manually change the week, day and month associations for the new year.

It got worse. The business also needed to keep last year's plan, so the legacy application carried two alternate time period hierarchies side by side. Two hierarchies meant two sets of manual changes, and more room for mistakes.

## The problem: a design that needed a person every year

Nothing was broken, exactly. But every year-end came with:

1. **Manual edits to a rigid hierarchy.** Re-associating days to weeks and weeks to months, by hand.
2. **A second hierarchy to keep in step.** The alternate one for last year's plan needed the same care.
3. **Risk.** A wrong association in a time hierarchy quietly puts data in the wrong period.

That was a lot of recurring effort to carry into a brand new platform.

## The question that changed everything

Before copying anything, we went back to the reporting requirement and asked a simple question: **at what level do you actually report?**

The answer was weeks. Nobody reported by day. The Day level existed because the legacy design had it, not because the business needed it.

That one clarification changed the design. Without days, we didn't need a deep, rigid hierarchy at all.

## The fix

We stopped treating the calendar as structure and started treating it as data.

**1. Weeks and months in separate dimensions.** Instead of nesting one inside the other, each got its own dimension. No day level, and no parent-child chain to re-wire every January.

**2. A custom table for the mapping.** The relationship between weeks and months lives in a custom table, not in a hierarchy. It holds which week belongs to which month.

**3. Business rules that use the mapping in both directions.**

- When data is loaded **by week**, a business rule reads the mapping and associates it with the correct month.
- When data is loaded **by month**, a business rule uses the same mapping to allocate it to the correct weeks.

Users can load at whichever level their source gives them, and both views stay in step.

**4. Automate the mapping itself.** The week-to-month mapping is generated by a scheduled job that runs at the start of the new year. Nobody has to prepare it by hand.

Because the associations are rows in a table and not branches in a hierarchy, the need for two alternate time hierarchies went away too. Keeping last year's plan became a data question, not a structural one.

## What changed

- **No year-end hierarchy edits.** The task that used to happen every December is gone.
- **No second hierarchy to maintain.** The alternate hierarchy for last year's plan is no longer needed.
- **Less risk.** A scheduled, repeatable process replaced manual changes that were easy to get wrong.
- **A simpler model.** No Day members, so fewer members to maintain and navigate.

## What I took from it

- **Revisit the reporting requirement.** Ask what level the business reports at today, not what level the old system stored. Detail that nobody reports on is just maintenance.
- **A migration is a chance to challenge the design.** Don't rebuild a structure only because it existed. Ask why it was built that way.
- **Look for recurring manual work.** If someone has to touch a hierarchy every year, that's a sign the design is carrying something it shouldn't.
- **Keep volatile relationships out of hierarchies.** Things that change every year, like week-to-month associations, are better as data in a table than as structure.
- **Automate the upkeep.** Even a good design is only as low-maintenance as the process around it. Scheduling the mapping refresh closed the last manual gap.
- **The effort pays back every year.** Asking the question took a conversation. The saving repeats at every year-end.

